# Boxes

A loom holds more than prose and code. An author sketches a diagram, leaves a note for a reader, and keeps the command that starts a server. An author also meets thoughts too large for the section they start in, and points to a chapter or a discussion of their own. An author writes every one of these as the same block, the box, and a trope can teach Loom a new kind of box without any new syntax.

The note is the only block of this kind today. It opens with `:::[Note] {}`, closes with a lone `:::`, and the classifier keeps every line between the two inert. A box grows the note into a general block with a name, a kind, parameters, and a body, and a box can hold other boxes.

## The shape of a box

A box opens with a line of three or more colons, then an optional name, then optional braces. It closes with a line of exactly as many colons as opened it.

```
::: One turn of the game {Mermaid}
flowchart LR
  Player -->|steps on| Cell
  Cell -->|wakes| Monster
:::
```

The name identifies the box in the book, the way a heading's title identifies a section. The name is also the box's caption, which the editor and the woven page show with the box. A box therefore never repeats its name in a parameter.

The braces hold the box's parameters. Each parameter is a `key=value` pair, and a value that holds a space is quoted. The parameter named `kind` gives the box its kind.

A bare word in the braces is shorthand for the kind, so `{Mermaid}` means `{kind=Mermaid}`. The shorthand reads like the `{Bash}` specifier on a section's heading, and a box almost always names its kind this way. Braces name the kind only once. A second bare word, or a bare word beside a `kind` parameter, is a fault.

A box with no kind is a note. The `{Note}` specifier names that kind outright, but a note needs no braces at all. A line of colons alone closes the open box whose opener has the same length, and anywhere else the same line opens a note.

A box inside another box opens with one colon more than its parent. The inner box's closer is then too long to close the outer box, so the two can never be confused. Here a note holds a diagram:

```
::: Why a grid
The room is small enough to model as cells, so the game needs no engine.

:::: Cells of the room {Mermaid}
flowchart LR
  Room --> Cell
  Cell --> Thing
::::
:::
```

The body of a box never composes. A line that starts with `#`, `=>`, or `~` inside a box opens nothing in the loom, just as a fence or a note keeps such lines inert today. The box's kind alone decides what its body means.

## Boxes that point to looms

A question about how to model a room may need a chapter of its own, and a question nobody has settled may need a discussion. The author then writes a box whose kind names the loom it points to, `{Chapter}` or `{Discussion}`, and whose name is that loom's title.

```
## Room

The room is 1000 steps wide and 700 steps long.

::: Room's 2d system {Chapter}
How to model the room without a game engine: a grid of cells and the things that occupy them.
:::
```

Such a box moves the opposite way to an anchor. An anchor pulls a section's code into the place where the anchor stands. A box that points to a loom pushes a thought out of the text, into a loom of its own, and leaves a short account of the thought behind.

The loom may not exist yet. The box then makes a promise, which the chapter "Names through the book" describes: a reference to the box resolves, and the editor marks it as promised until the loom is written. When the author opens the loom, the editor writes it with the box's name as its title and the box's body as its opening prose. A new chapter starts with the status `Canvas`, which the chapter "Canvases" describes.

Once the loom exists, the box's body is the loom's summary. For a chapter, the summary is the opening prose under the chapter's title. For a discussion, it is the opening prose followed by the discussion's conclusions. The loom holds the summary's source, and the box holds a copy.

The editor keeps the two in step. An edit to either one reaches the other, and the author can open the box in a side panel to edit the loom's opening without leaving the section. Loom's command line refreshes every copy from its source, the way `loom toc` regenerates the table of contents from the chapters' frontmatter. A copy that differs from its source is a warning, never a silent overwrite, so an edit made outside the editor is never lost.

A settled discussion therefore reads in its chapter as a few paragraphs of prose, followed by an arrow that leads to the argument behind them.

A section that holds a box of kind `Chapter` holds no code. The box marks where a chapter of its own begins, so the code it promises belongs in that chapter. A chapter box in a section with code is a fault. A discussion box may sit beside code, since a discussion often argues about the code beside it.

## Kinds come from tropes

The kinds of box form an open set, as languages do. The core knows four kinds, because Loom itself gives them their meaning:

- **Note:** a remark for the reader, and the kind of every box that names none.
- **Chapter:** a box that points to a chapter.
- **Discussion:** a box that points to a discussion.
- **Conclusion:** the answer that closes a topic in a discussion, which the chapter "Discussions" describes. A conclusion anywhere else is a fault.

Every other kind comes from a trope, the package the chapter "Tropes" defines. A trope that contributes a kind says how the editor draws a box of that kind, whether the box runs, and what the box's body must satisfy. Two such kinds come first:

- **Mermaid:** a diagram written in Mermaid, which the editor and the woven page draw from the box's body.
- **Run:** a command, which the editor runs on request, showing its output beside the box.

A kind that neither the core nor an installed trope knows is a fault, so a misspelt kind never passes for content.

## Notes become boxes

Today's note opener, `:::[Note] {}`, becomes a line of three colons alone, or `::: {Note}` for an author who wants to name the kind. The note keeps its body and its closer, and the rewrite over the book is mechanical. The classifier's `inNote` flag grows into a stack of open boxes, one entry for each depth, so a closer ends exactly the box whose length it matches.

## What to work out

- **The woven page.** What a reader of the woven book sees of a box that points to a loom, the summary, a link, or both, is open.
- **The note's braces.** Whether anything the braces in `:::[Note] {}` carry today must survive as a parameter of a note is open.
- **How far a box's name reaches,** an open question the chapter "Names through the book" shares.
- **The commands that write boxes,** which the chapter "Editor commands" collects.

## What this chapter grows into

1. The box in the classifier, the tokeniser, and the Loom AST: its opener, name, parameters, kind and the kind's shorthand, body, and nesting by the length of its fence.
2. Every note in the book rewritten as a box with no kind.
3. Boxes that point to looms: found by name, opened as a chapter or a discussion, their summaries kept in step with the loom's opening prose, and the fault for a chapter box beside code.
4. The registry of kinds: `Note`, `Chapter`, `Discussion`, and `Conclusion` in the core, and the kinds tropes contribute, with `Mermaid` and `Run` first.
5. Boxes in the tree-sitter grammar and in each editor's highlighting.

When boxes parse, nest, and draw in the editor, this chapter joins Part II, The shape of a loom, and Part III, Reading the text.
